      "costUsd": 0.0079485,
      "pronunciationText": "Last video, we found areas by turning the strips sideways. Now picture a garden path being levelled. Along two metres, the ground's height above the planned level is x cubed cos x metres, a mound at one end and a hollow at the other. The net soil is the integral from minus one to one of x cubed cos x. No rule in this course gives its primitive. Yet its value is exactly zero. The soil cut from the mound exactly fills the hollow. Look at the graph. What do you notice about its two halves?"
    },
