      "possibleRetry": false,
      "inputTokens": 143,
      "outputTokens": 961,
      "costUsd": 0.0087205,
      "pronunciationText": "If there's no primitive and no symmetry, we estimate, and trapezia gave the best estimates in the strips video. Each trapezium's area is half the strip width, times its two heights added together. Now add four trapezia, side by side. Each inside height is an edge of two trapezia, so it's counted twice. The two end heights belong to one trapezium each, so they're counted once. That gives the trapezoidal rule: h over two, times the first height, plus two times every inside height, plus the last height. Here h is the strip width, b minus a, over n, and n strips need n plus one heights."
    },
